  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nA nearly stationary front is located from near Brainerd to\nWillmar and Canby at 2 PM. Temperatures have risen back into the\n80s east of the front, with 70s to the west. Some readings\nacross southern MN are approaching 90. STC and EAU are within a\nfew degrees of breaking their record highs and MSP has already\nbroke the record high of 85 with 86 around 145 PM. Highs late \nthis afternoon will be about 3 or 4 degrees warmer than current\nreadings.\n\nThe front will very slowly sink south tonight and become\nstationary again Monday from southwest MN to northwest WI. A\nfew weak areas of low pressure will track along the front late\ntonight and Monday. Mid and high level clouds will increase this\nevening, but no precipitation is expected through tonight. As\nthe main low approaches from the southwest, a few showers and \nthunderstorms may develop along and north of the front in the \nafternoon. Early day cloudiness is expected to decrease some \nduring the morning, allowing for sufficient heating. The \nboundary layer will remain quite dry and temperatures aloft will\nbe warm, which should limit coverage of any development. The \ntemperature contract along the front won't be as impressive as \ntoday across the Dakotas, but it will be noticeably cooler \nnorth with highs in the 70s vs 80s to the south. The low will \ntrack across southeast MN Monday evening when the front will \nclear the area to the south. Highs by Tuesday will range from \nthe 50s across northern and west central MN, to upper 60s across\nsouthern MN. \n\nA large trough and upper low over the Rockies midweek will track\neast Wednesday night and Thursday. Meanwhile, another trough\nwill be diving southeast across the Great Lakes Thursday night\nand Friday. There remains a large range of solutions how these\ntwo features will interact, but the trend has been toward a\ndrier forecast with phasing occurring east of the region. \nEven cooler air will follow through Saturday. Widespread 20s and\n30s for lows Friday night may end the growing season for many.\nAnother warming trend will follow Sunday and into early next\nweek with highs returning back into the 60s and perhaps 70s.",
